    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d724">

                <pb facs="0622" n="610" sameAs="frus:frus1942v04/pg610"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">890E.01/142: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Beirut</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gwynn</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Beirut</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-08-12T13:00:00+02:00">August 12, 1942—1
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received August 13—3:19 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>281. Reference paragraph 2 of my 280.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>Monsieur Baelen, Catroux’s Chef du Cabinet Politique told me that a letter
                        has just been received from Casey urging the necessity of holding elections
                        in the immediate future in Syria and Lebanon and that Casey had said the
                        United States Government had indicated that it too desired these elections.
                        I asked Baelen if he could give me a verbatim extract of Casey’s letter. He
                        said that he would have to ask Catroux’s permission but thought it would not
                        be refused. I will wire it immediately if obtainable.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>In view of the ideas I have expressed, I should greatly appreciate being
                        informed if there is any basis of truth in Casey’s reported
                        statement.</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>If elections should be held, I think it a foregone conclusion that they
                        will be not only anti-French but anti-Ally unless they are very actively
                        manipulated.</item>
                </list>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gwynn</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
